{'parameter_id': 3, 'term_id': 'TERM-001', 'status': 'active', 'merged_into': None, 'notes': 'Re-run of the cleared circulation corpus. TERM-001 is base vocabulary minted 2026-05-09, so this is a PROMOTION, not a coining: add-term/observe-term is deliberately not used, which would have coined a near-duplicate beside the existing term.', 'created_at': '2026-09-16 04:55', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-16-research-batch-08-ramp-gradient', 'updated_at': '2026-09-17 22:13', 'updated_by_session': 'session_2026-09-17-engine-proxy-inference', 'direction_rationale': 'A gentler ramp is more accessible, and the admitted evidence states it rather than the engine assuming it. REF-00996 (Handboek Toegankelijkheid) gives both the criterion and its mechanism: hoe groter het hoogteverschil, hoe zachter de helling moet zijn, omdat men gedurende een langere tijd een inspanning moet leveren — the greater the rise, the gentler the ramp must be, because the effort must be sustained longer — with the physical feasibility of the independently self-propelling wheelchair user as the starting point. REF-00987 (ADA) says it in its own advisory to 405.2: provide ramps with the least possible running slope. REF-00994 (IPC) frames 1:20 as best practice and 1:14 as a concession for ancillary facilities only, which is the same direction stated as a preference ordering. The T1 dose-response rows measure it: articular discomfort and pushrim force both rise with gradient across the tested range. Four sources, three strata, one direction, none contradicted — derived, not adjudicated. NOT contested: no admitted source identifies a population for whom a steeper ramp is better. Recorded because compose_value cannot select most-accommodatingly without it and was returning a NULL interval with that exact complaint.', 'accessibility_direction': 'lower_is_better'}

{'parameter_id': 12, 'term_id': 'TERM-091', 'status': 'active', 'merged_into': None, 'notes': "Promoted from the base vocabulary on the owner's direction of 2026-10-09 (populate the parameter table from the existing terms registry). Judged a measurable design quantity by the session; no source adjudication behind it. Direction left unset until an admitted source states which way is better for a disabled person. || ANNOTATED 2026-10-30 by session_2026-10-30-rest-landing-parameter: Direction is now recorded (accessibility_direction, with its rationale in direction_rationale), so 'Direction left unset until an admitted source states which way is better' above no longer holds. This is the parameter the owner's 2026-10-30 'rest landing' hypothesis (references/project-standards.md, owner rulings 2026-10-30, item 8) names: the maximum run between rests IS the run of a flight between landings, so no second parameter was minted (rule 5). The vertical expression of the same limit is a separate open question (see the gap filed this session). GAP-067 stays open for parameters 4 to 11 and for the other false sentence here.", 'created_at': '2026-10-09 05:50', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-10-09-research-batch-24-parameter-pilot', 'updated_at': '2026-10-30 21:49', 'updated_by_session': 'session_2026-10-30-rest-landing-parameter', 'direction_rationale': "A shorter permitted run between landings is more accessible for a disabled person: the admitted sources state the need a landing serves, and none states a longer unbroken run as better. REF-00992 (Approved Document M, para 1.22): 'Some people need to be able to stop frequently; for instance to regain strength or breath, or to ease pain.' REF-00995 (Flemish decree of 5 June 2009, explanatory memorandum to Article 19): a landing is required per 50 cm of level difference or 10 m of ramp 'om de nodige rustpunten te voorzien door te vermijden dat een helling te lang aanhoudt' (to provide the necessary rest points by preventing a ramp continuing too long). REF-00996 gives the same mechanism: a gradient is tied to a maximum length 'zodat voldoende rustpunten ingebouwd worden'. Derived from those statements, not adjudicated; no source states the direction as a rule about the run limit itself. NOT contested: no admitted source identifies a population for whom a longer unbroken run is better. The codes pair a shorter run with a steeper permitted gradient (REF-00992 Table 1); that trade belongs to the gradient parameter (3, lower_is_better), not to this one.", 'accessibility_direction': 'lower_is_better'}

